Singapore’s tourism industry has made a strong recovery, with the number of inbound tourists increasing by 19 times compared to 2021.
(Combining reports from the 11th): The Singapore Tourism Board recently announced that it expects the number of international visitors to Singapore to reach between 12 and 14 million in 2023, generating tourism revenues of approximately S$18 to S$21 billion. The number of visitors and tourism revenue are expected to return to around 67% to 75% of the levels prior to the COVID-19 pandemic in 2019. There is a high likelihood that the tourism industry will fully recover in 2024.
The tourism market in Singapore gradually restarted and recovered in 2022.
The latest statistics released by the Singapore Tourism Board show that the number of inbound visitors in 2022 increased by 19 times compared to 2021, rising from 330,600 to 6.3 million. This exceeds the board’s earlier estimate of 4 to 6 million visitors, and represents a recovery to 33% of the levels seen before the pandemic.
Tourism revenue in Singapore for January to September 2022 was S$8.96 billion, and it is expected that tourism revenue for the full year of 2022 will be between S$13.8 billion and S$14.3 billion, up sixfold to sevenfold from S$1.89 billion in 2021, which has already recovered to around 50% to 52% of pre-COVID-19 levels.
The Singapore government is taking multiple measures to accelerate the recovery of the tourism industry.
In April 2022, the government announced the allocation of nearly 500 million Singapore dollars to stimulate the recovery of the tourism industry.
The Tourism Authority recently announced that it plans to allocate S$110 million from this budget over the next two years to launch more business and leisure activities aimed at attracting tourists back.
In order to make wellness tourism one of the highlights of Singapore's tourist offerings, the Singapore Tourism Board launched a public consultation campaign on November 23, 2022, with the aim of developing a wellness destination along the southern coast of Singapore.
Wang Lingli, the Head of the Sports and Wellness Tourism Division at the Singapore Tourism Board, said that in recent years, an increasing number of tourists have placed their physical health at the top of their list of considerations when making travel decisions.
Developing a health and wellness attraction in the southern area of Binhai, which includes the Binhai Bay Gardens and the Binhai Causeway, is expected to contribute to Singapore's tourism industry and economy. This will encourage tourists to spend more money in Singapore and stay for a longer period of time.
Chen Jianlong, the Director of Tourism at Singapore, stated that in the coming years, health and wellness will become an important part of Singapore's tourism offerings.
The COVID-19 pandemic has raised public awareness and demand for both physical and mental health.
For this reason, over the past two years, the Tourism Authority has been promoting health and wellness programs to both the local public and international tourists.
In June 2022, the Singapore Tourism Board organized the first Health and Wellness Festival, offering the public more than 130 various health-related activities and experiences.
Singapore will continue to offer new travel experiences to visitors.
In addition to the newly opened Singapore Children’s Museum, Avatar World at Marina Bay Gardens, the Central Beach Market in Sentosa, the “Exploring New Frontiers” exhibition hall at the Arts and Sciences Museum, and the newly built outdoor theater at the Night Wildlife Park, the Tourism Board will support the development and renewal of attractions such as the Bird Park at Wild Wild Life. This will help make Singapore an attractive international travel destination.
According to the plan, Singapore will establish industry standards for the sustainable development of the conference, exhibition, and rewards tourism sector by the end of 2023. The focus will be on improving waste management and carbon emissions, with the aim of further enhancing the tourism environment.
The recovery of Singapore's tourism industry has accelerated the recovery of the aviation sector as well.
Data shows that as of December 18, 2022, passenger traffic at Changi Airport has strongly recovered, with up to 150,000 passengers being handled daily and approximately 1 million passengers per week. This represents around 75% of the levels prior to the COVID-19 pandemic. The number of aviation industry employees also has returned to about 90% of its pre-pandemic levels.
Data shows that from January to October 2022, Singapore welcomed 23.6 million visitors, which is 42% of the level before the COVID-19 pandemic. Additionally, there were 170,000 flight departures and arrivals, representing 53% of the number of flights during the pre-pandemic period.
The tourism industry has recovered rapidly, which in turn has accelerated the recovery of related sectors such as accommodation, transportation, dining, and shopping.
The Singaporean government and the tourism industry have high hopes for the Chinese market.
Koh Shu Dan, Assistant Director of the International Department at the Singapore Tourism Board, said that a survey by a Chinese travel platform ranked Singapore’s safety index among the top three in the world, which is one of the reasons why Singapore is so popular among Chinese tourists.
Before the pandemic, China welcomed about 3 million visitors to Singapore for travel and tourism every year.
As China relaxes its border policies, it is expected that the number of Chinese tourists this year could recover to 30% to 60% of the level in 2019.
Ke Shudan stated that in the future, the Tourism Bureau will closely monitor any further signals from China regarding policies related to outbound travel, in order to respond to the changing needs of tourists.
In addition to strengthening partnerships with Chinese online travel platforms such as Ctrip and travel agencies, the Singapore Tourism Board will actively prepare to welcome Chinese tourists in response to the new trends in travel outside of China.
Looking ahead to the tourism market in 2023, the Singapore Tourism Board is confident that the recovery of the tourism industry will be faster than expected, provided that there is no significant global economic recession that affects public incomes, and no new large-scale and deadly pandemics occur.
An introductory book for quickly understanding internet finance
This book is very shallow; it can be considered an introductory course that will help you quickly understand the broad picture of internet finance but to really delve deeper, you need to follow the map and dig on your own.
And given that it was published earlier, and the content about later on in the war between Alipay and WeChat Pay for subsidies has not been mentioned yet, the share of third-party payments now is unlikely to be as small as what is stated in the book.
The bank has also been criticized for its lack of transparency and transparency, and the fact that many people are taking their phones out to eat, and businessmen are abandoning the bank because of the high bank liquidity.
But still, I am very grateful to the author for writing this introductory book; it is evident that the author has had many years of financial observation experience and is familiar with both domestic and international financial development histories, which allows him/her to write in such an easy-to-understand manner.
It’s easy to write about grand, sweeping themes; the difficult part is to make the writing accessible and easy to understand for the general audience.
