I read this book in my second year of high school, just out of boredom. I thought I wouldn’t get too engrossed in it, considering that online novels in general haven’t really attracted me much in the past.
Then... he fell into the pit.
According to this plot, the whole book is quite light and enjoyable. In the earlier parts, when Ye Xiu is still playing in the online game, even the scheming and power struggles between guilds don’t become too serious because Ye Xiu’s antics always make things seem comical or absurd.
As for the part about the actual competition, I expressed a slight sense of sadness regarding the changes of the times and the transition from the old to the new (maybe I’m just being too sentimental).
It took me a lot of study time during my second year of high school to read the entire book, but I just couldn’t stop.
The in-depth portrayal of characters and their emotional relationships in “Full-time” is truly remarkable. Even characters that are only briefly mentioned have a profound impact on the story. Key characters like Ye Xiu, Huang Shaotian, and Su Mucheng have a huge number of fans, while those who are not at the center of the narrative, such as Jiang Botao and Chu Yunxiu, are also very likable. It’s astonishing that, within a story set in the world of esports, there isn’t a single character who is truly detestable. This is a testament to the success of “Full-time” in terms of character development.
On the emotional front, being a full-time player doesn’t incur the problems associated with the typical online novels, such as having an enormous harem of thirty million women—either all beautiful or of a perverted nature. The emotions of all the characters in the story share one common aspect: they all love the game “Glory,” and most of them desire to achieve a legitimate, honorable victory.
There aren’t any big conspiracies involved in the small measurements; everyone just looks so innocent and adorable.
This might also be why there aren’t any particularly annoying characters in the full-time roles.
The full-time position related to IP (Intellectual Property) is quite significant, and this is also thanks to that.
Putting aside the cultural phenomenon of online novels, “Full-Time” is indeed a decent book.
To some extent, he really manages to inspire people with his passion and bring a touch of emotion to them.
