I appreciate you, and I really want you to know that!
The primary factor that determines a person’s job satisfaction is not the amount of money they earn, but whether they feel that their work is valued and recognized sufficiently.
According to Maslow’s Hierarchy of Needs, once a person’s basic physiological needs are met, the most urgent need is the satisfaction of psychological needs—the desire to be understood, affirmed, and appreciated.
Indeed, for most people in that country, it’s difficult to express their love verbally; even when they feel love, gratitude, and appreciation, they keep them to themselves and never say anything out loud.
The author describes five types of expressive languages of appreciation in this book: affirmative words, thoughtful gestures, acts of service, pleasing gifts, and physical contact.
I think it is more important that you can express appreciation in these five languages without reservation and with sincerity whenever you encounter people around you, whether they be family members, friends, or colleagues.
Actually, the more closely we get to someone, the more we need appreciation and gratitude from them, making our lives better.
By learning to appreciate employees, companies can retain talent to a certain extent and reduce the turnover rate.
Thank you to the translators for bringing us wonderful foreign ideas and perspectives from around the world.
Due to cultural differences between the East and the West, there will always be some variations between languages. I believe that if one had the opportunity to see the original version written by the author, they would gain even more from it.
Once again, thank you to the authors and translators as well as editors for their hard work!
A customer’s confession
1. Upon receiving such a defective product, Amazon customer service told me not to worry and that they would contact me within about 54 hours.
2 Then in the middle you contact the customer service, they will tell you that the problem will be handled by a commissioner, who will inform you of the result.
You can never get in touch with this specialist on your own; you don’t know her employee ID, and you don’t have a direct phone number to call her. It’s only possible for her to contact you, not the other way around.
The tragedy is that after waiting for almost three days, the Commissioner contacted me, my phone was off for a moment and I did not receive the Commissioner's call.
I waited for two hours without getting that specialist's call; I then contacted Amazon customer service and sent an email to them asking her to call me back.
It’s been almost three days, and I still don’t know what Amazon will do about the defective coffee machine I received.
The products from Amazon in the US have no issues; purchasing goods from overseas is a great way to do it. However, the service provided by Amazon compared to that of local retailers in the US is far inferior.
6 For me, I can only keep waiting...
Not really recommended as reading material.
I read it a long time ago, and I happened to see the opportunity to write some reflections based on my memory.
This book is less of a collection of short stories than a collection of fragments from real life that deal with the theme of love.
The tone of this book is extremely plain—as plain as plain water. The choice of words and the construction of sentences have been pushed to the extreme of simplicity. The plot is just like the streets on a summer afternoon: quiet and uneventful.
Each short story is a snapshot of a small period in everyday life, without an opening or an ending.
Finally, all these elements were gathered together and given the title “What Are We Talking About When We Talk About Love?”
I think that this collection of novels, along with its title, serves as a form of performance art. The author wants to convey the idea that love is not merely about the highs and lows that occur on stage, or the enduring devotion depicted in urban legends; it’s also not just about the intense affection between two people. Love encompasses more than that, and should not be represented by those aspects alone. Love includes the mundane, everyday aspects of life, the tedious repetition of days, as well as feelings of helplessness in the face of life, the avoidance of reality, and the uncertainty about the future.
As you read this book, memories of couples arguing around you, the dull and uninteresting marriages of people you know, and unsatisfactory relationships may come to mind.
This is exactly why I don’t recommend reading it. From an aesthetic point of view, both the writing style and the plot are utterly dull. Unless your life is full of extreme highs and lows or as sweet as living in a candy castle, you’ll likely find the experience both predictable and boring.
And in terms of depth of thought, the everyday scenes do not offer anything particularly enlightening or that could broaden a person’s perspectives (which is different from García Márquez’s *Love in the Time of Cholera*). The brief segments neither explore any questions, such as what kind of mistakes led to the failure of the relationship.
What are the reasons behind the breakdown of the protagonist’s marriage?
So I don’t recommend reading this book; it doesn’t really make much sense whether you read it or not.
The author’s ideas are expressed more through techniques and genre elements than through the plot itself, which is also one of the reasons why the reading experience of this book is rather poor.
Is it broken?
What about the counterfeit goods?
In short, it was a tragedy.
When using the second one, I chose pores that were finer, but when it opened up, there was an odd smell, thinking it was normal because I had never used this product before. Then when I put it on my face, I couldn't take it anymore and felt very strong. The smell was so intense that I couldn't describe it. It made me cough immediately and my eyes hurt as well; I had to stop using it right away and wash my face afterwards.
Not believing in the claims, I bought another identical product. This one doesn’t have such a strong smell, and I’m currently using it as a mask.
It reminds me of the smell of low-quality hair straightening products used in hair salons.
It’s really hard to imagine that skincare products sold directly by Amazon will also have to be avoided from now on…
The experience is above average.
It’s almost like the top-of-the-line electric toothbrushes; there are many different cleaning modes available. However, few people actually change to a different mode every day when using their toothbrushes, right?
I’ve used it for half a year with no problems; I brush my teeth with it every day, and I only need to rinse it out about once every week or so.
Brush head accessories are just as cheap when bought on Amazon.
The app is a bit difficult to use. Apart from the issues related to color customization, do the other compatibility problems seem to be due to the fact that it’s an overseas version?
Hope to learn about the great Dharma.
Garbage after-sales service
The piano I bought one and a half months ago is missing a part. After notifying Amazon, I was given a phone number for after-sales support. Despite several attempts to communicate with them, I still haven’t received the part yet. What kind of efficiency is this?!
I guess that if there are problems after the purchase and they need to be repaired, it will be even more troublesome. I suggest that friends who value good after-sales service should just look for other options; it’s really too much of a hassle!
The merchant considered the customer’s needs before shipping the product :)
