Strong recommendation that Amazon stop using EMS!
The product is five stars, logistics one star, average three stars.
I don’t understand why Amazon would use EMS for shipping; it’s widely recognized in the industry as an unreliable method. After all, a computer is a valuable item!
After making the purchase, the order showed that delivery was expected on the 20th. So, our family members waited at home for three days!
Why does it take four to five days to send something from Xiamen to Fuzhou?
This morning on the 22nd, I checked the logistics tracking and finally saw that the package had arrived in Fuzhou. I was ready to happily receive it, but in the evening—without receiving any phone calls or texts—I checked the tracking again and found that the package had already been signed for at noon, and it even showed that I had signed for it myself!
I was shocked in an instant!
Who am I? Where am I? Did I really buy this computer?
I’m just starting to doubt the whole meaning of life, you know?
I had to make one last trip to a collection point before I could get the computer back.
The specific reason, in simple terms, is that the delivery driver mixed up the two order numbers. Fortunately, the items weren’t lost, so that’s the main thing.
I strongly recommend that Amazon stop using EMS for deliveries. If Amazon continues to use EMS for shipping packages in the future, I will definitely not buy anything from them again.
Waiting for a response from the supplier.
The packaging had no markings at all. I directly called the supplier and was told that the absence of markings indicated that the product was genuine. If I had any doubts, I could send the product (including any spare parts) to Johnson & Johnson for inspection by mail. I also provided my vehicle license plate number and phone number, and they promised to send me a text message to inform me that they had entered my information into Johnson & Johnson’s official warranty database. However, I still haven’t received that text message to this day…
Japan's Amazon has severely harmed buyers' interests due to a customer service issue (the 8c caterpillar shipped as an 8cm caterpillar) (8c = 14cm; 8cm = 2c).
Admitting the mistake, but constantly shirking responsibility by emphasizing that the goods belong to a third party and that one cannot impose any obligations on that third party.
Order number: 503-4265464-1830254.
The size options of the tape measure I purchased were 7c, 8, and 9c. I immediately felt that this was a trick. I called the Japanese customer service in Chinese and was clearly informed that “8” referred to 8c. However, when the product arrived in China, I found that the actual length was only 8 cm. I believe this was a deliberate attempt to deceive Chinese customers (according to Amazon’s after-sales policy). Now, when I check the product page, these size options are no longer available.
Chinese Customer Service: We apologize for the inconvenience caused to you. We have two options for you:
1. If you return the product, the buyer is responsible for all shipping costs.
2. If you don’t return the product, we will compensate you with 1000 yen.
I calculate it; shoes cost 4000+ yen, which is about 200 RMB. Option 1: I don't have shoes, so there's a loss of 200 yuan.
Choose option 2; I had shoes and was compensated with 2,000 yen, resulting in a loss of 1,500 RMB. The customer service replied that they would record it, handle it seriously, and send an email back the next day stating that I did not accept the mediation from the Chinese customer service, which ended up this way.
I mentioned that on the purchase page, there were confusing options like 7c, 8c, 9c. The Chinese customer service staff helped me by explaining that the Japanese standard codes don’t use the letter “c”; instead, they use numbers like 140. But have you even checked the other related pages (like the “caterpillar”-related ones)?
Both US and Japanese size labels are sometimes used interchangeably.
Besides, why have you placed the 8cm ruler between the 7cm and 9cm rulers? It’s clearly the smallest size of all; what does it mean to put it there?
Chinese customer service: We provide services in Chinese, but since we don’t know that you are shipping the item back to your home country, we will not cover the shipping costs for you.
I don’t understand this logic anymore. I’m Japanese, and I still need Chinese-language customer support. Chinese people living in Japan don’t need Chinese-language customer support either. Tourists who come to Japan don’t have the time to make international long-distance calls. So the question is: Who exactly needs to make Chinese-language calls to Amazon’s overseas shopping services? It’s probably people like me who shop on Japanese online platforms.
Isn’t the Chinese customer service on Amazon just there to make it easier for us people who shop overseas?
The packaging is damaged; we need to wait for some time to assess the quality of the product.
The outer packaging is severely damaged; the inner box seems to be new, but the instruction manual does not appear to be new. The house was renovated in September, and we are stocking up on products. I hope they will not be damaged by the time they are delivered. Please explain to Amazon why several customers have mentioned the packaging damage, yet you seem to be unbothered by this issue. Thank you!
East and west are good, but platform bureaucrats still exist.
